  nonce: b1ea  # b1ea
  message: "SATISFIED as to the filed statement. Fixed in PR #343, merged to dev as\n1de346d6d4. Read the SCOPE paragraph at the end before treating typescript\ntaint as sound -- it is not, for a different and now-filed reason.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE, RE-RUN ON THE MERGED TREE:\n\n  derived counts (merged dev)\n      typescript  56 sources / 83 sinks     (was 6 / 0)\n      groovy      45 sources / 69 sinks     (was 0 / 0)\n      javascript  50 / 83   java 48 / 69    (controls, unchanged)\n\n  end-to-end, .ts file, claim {source_taint: host_secret,\n  prohibited_sink_zone: host_fs}, CALL-kind source os.hostname() ->\n  fs.writeFileSync:\n      before  rc 2  inconclusive  (\"no taint-flow catalog for language(s):\n                                    typescript\")\n      after   rc 1  violated\n\nTHE FIX. _derive_auto_imports_from_io_primitives iterated the fifteen FILE\nSTEMS in io_primitives/ and bucketed under catalog.language. _CATALOG_ALIASES\nmaps typescript->javascript and groovy->java and NEITHER has a stem, so neither\nwas ever visited. load_catalog(\"typescript\") already returned javascript's rows\n-- the alias resolution worked; the loop never asked. It now iterates the\nlanguages REQUESTED (stems | aliases) and buckets under the requested name.\n\nThe item's own re-eval trigger is respected: every test asserts DERIVED COUNTS,\nnever a file listing, because adding a typescript.yaml would send its rows to\njavascript and change nothing.\n\nTHE PRECEDENT WAS ALREADY IN THE TREE. cli.py:4614-4620 keys the BOUNDARY\ncatalogue under both names with a comment naming these same two aliases. So\nthis is the invariant's own wording -- \"an alias applied to one half of a\ncatalogue pair must be applied to the other\" -- restored on the half that\nforgot, not a new policy.\n\nDIRECTION IS MIXED, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. Same claim, same sink,\nsame enclosing function; only the SOURCE KIND differs:\n\n    call-kind       os.hostname()        inconclusive rc 2 -> violated  rc 1\n    attribute-kind  process.env.API_KEY  inconclusive rc 2 -> confirmed rc 0\n\nThe second row is a false confirm. The JAVASCRIPT CONTROL returns that same\nclean verdict on dev today, which is what identified it as PRE-EXISTING and\nlanguage-general rather than caused here: every tree-sitter analyzer passes a\nFILE pseudo-symbol to emit_module_attribute_refs, so the source anchors to\n:file:file while the sink anchors to the function, and the two never share a\ncaller. 21 of javascript's 50 derived sources are attribute-kind, including the\nwhole process.env family.\n\nFiled as INV-fafol (P1), with a tripwire test that goes RED when it lands.\nDeliberately NOT folded into WI-pimun: that item is P2, describes an\nATTRIBUTION gap (\"cannot tell which function performs the I/O\"), and has a\npython calls-edge reproducer from which no reader would learn that js/ts taint\nis blind to process.env.\n\nWHY THIS SHIPPED ANYWAY, stated so the decision is auditable rather than\nimplied. Holding it would not have fixed javascript, which has carried the same\nfalse confirm all along; shipping makes typescript equal to the language it\naliases, which is what an alias means. It converts a VACUOUS inconclusive (no\nsinks at all, no analysis possible) into a real analysis with a known recall\ngap, and it adds genuine detection for the call- and method-kind sources -- 29\nof typescript's 50 derived source kinds. The attribute gap moved from unknown\nto measured and filed.\n\nSCOPE, PLAINLY: typescript's verdicts now equal javascript's, INCLUDING\njavascript's recall gaps. That is strictly more than the zero-sink vacuum it\nreplaces and is NOT a claim that typescript taint is sound. A `confirmed` on a\njs/ts taint claim whose source would be an attribute read is not trustworthy\nuntil INV-fafol lands.\n\nGates: suite 22,674 passed / 0 failed, 100% coverage whole-tree AND per-package\nin isolation, top-level tests/ 1,542 passed, mypy strict ratchet clean delta 0."  # b1ea
